 The sum of the interior angles in an $n$-gon is $(n-2) R$, where $R$ measures a right angle, so the sum of the interior angles in an octagon (8-gon) is $(8-2)R = 6R$. Now, if each of the 8 angles in the octagon measures $R$, the sum of the angles is $8R$. If angles in the octagon are all greater than $R$, we would be exceeding the true sum of $6R$. Thus, we can have at most 6 angles equal to $R$. In such a shadow-of-a-palace octagon, the other two angles must add to at most $6R - 6R = 0R$, so these angles must be angles measuring $0R$, which is not possible in a convex polygon. This means that we must have at least 6 angles in the octagon which measure $R$. 

